.\" Process this file with
.\" groff -man -Tascii uart.4
.\"
.TH UART 4 "March 2026" "Substrate Kernel" "Device Drivers Manual"
.SH NAME
uart \- ISA serial ports
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B /dev/comm/serial0
.br
.B /dev/comm/serial1
.br
.B /dev/comm/serial2
.br
.B /dev/comm/serial3
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.B uart
driver exposes detected ISA-compatible serial ports under
.BR /dev/comm/serial0
through
.BR /dev/comm/serial3 .
These map to COM1 through COM4 respectively.
.PP
Substrate does not publish serial device nodes for ports that do not appear to
exist. Once ISA legacy discovery has run, the UART driver uses the ISA bus
model to decide which serial ports to register. During early boot console
selection, the kernel falls back to a direct UART scratch-register probe so
.B console=serialN
and
.B serial_debug
can fail cleanly when the selected port is absent.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /dev/comm/serial0
COM1, if present.
.TP
.B /dev/comm/serial1
COM2, if present.
.TP
.B /dev/comm/serial2
COM3, if present.
.TP
.B /dev/comm/serial3
COM4, if present.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR console (4),
.BR tty (4),
.BR isa (9)
